{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-81409",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-0{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=0.5.$ \n\nPlugging $x=0.5$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|0.5-0{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.5{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,0.5].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-{e}^{-x}|={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|={e}^{-x}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,0.5],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(0)|=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{1}{6}|0.5{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot {2}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
